UCAPI Property: covdbInstParameters
The usage of the covdbClassDefinitions property is as follows:
covdbHandle defs = covdb_iterate(_design, covdbClassDefinitions);
covdbHandle def;
while((def = covdb_scan(defs)))
{
printf("class name: %s\n",
covdb_get_str(def, covdbName));
}
covdb_release_handle(defs);
UCAPI Property: covdbInstParameters
The covdbInstParameters relation enables applications to iterate over module
parameters associated with a specific source instance in a coverage database. Use this
relation to extract parameter names and values from coverage database (VDB) instances;
for example, when comparing parameters between simulation and formal verification
databases to detect mismatches that can cause coverage mapping inconsistencies.
To dump instance parameters into the design XML, pass the -cm_instr
dump_inst_params option to the compile-time command. UCAPI can then extract these
parameters using the covdbInstParameters relation. Instance parameters are available
for iteration through UCAPI only if the VDB was created with this option.
The usage of the covdbInstParameters relation is as follows:
covdbHandle instIter = covdb_iterate(design, covdbDefinitions);
covdbHandle inst;
while ((inst = covdb_scan(instIter)) != NULL) {
char* instName = covdb_get_str(inst, covdbName);
covdbHandle instanceIter = covdb_iterate(inst, covdbInstances);
covdbHandle instance;
while ((instance = covdb_scan(instanceIter)) != NULL) {
covdbHandle paramIter = covdb_iterate(instance,
covdbInstParameters);
covdbHandle paramHandle;
printf("\n\n%s\n", covdb_get_str(instance, covdbFullName));
while ((paramHandle = covdb_scan(paramIter)) != NULL) {
printf("%s\n", covdb_get_str(paramHandle, covdbName));
printf("%s\n", covdb_get_str(paramHandle, covdbValue));
}
covdb_release_handle(paramIter);
}
covdb_release_handle(instanceIter);
}
Each parameter handle returned by the covdbInstParameters iterator returns
parameters specific to that instance.

UCAPI Property: covdbLibName
Parameter values are returned as formatted strings. For example, parameter <name> =
<value> (parameter WIDTH = 32 and parameter DEPTH = 8 ). Use covdbName for the
parameter name and covdbValue for the parameter value.
If non-integer parameter support is needed, use the covdbValueSet handle type instead.
UCAPI Property: covdbLibName
If a module or covergroup is compiled into a library in the currently-loaded VDB, you can
use the covdbLibName property to get the name of that library.
For example, if a covergroup with handle grpHdl is shown in the report as LIB1.pkg::cg,
calling covdb_get_str(grpHdl, covdbLibName) returns the string LIB1.
Loading a Design
Note that when using VCS, designs are loaded with the covdb_load() function. The
following function, loadDesign, accepts a string argument representing the path to the
design and then loads the design.
covdbHandle loadDesign(char *design)
{
covdbHandle designHandle = covdb_load(covdbDesign, NULL, design);
if(!designHandle)
printf("Error: could not load design %s\n", design);
printf("Info: loaded design %s\n", design);
return designHandle;
}
// example of calling the function:
covdbHandle designHandle = loadDesign("simv"); //
A handle returned by covdb_load is persistent until unloaded by
covdb_unload.
Available Tests
Loading a design does not load the test(s) associated with a design. Given a valid design
handle, the available tests can be accessed.
Calling covdb_iterate() with covdbAvailableTests returns an iterator to a list of the
testnames associated with the design. These tests are not automatically loaded; they must
be loaded with one of the API functions. The following C function shows the available tests
associated with a design handle.

Loading a Design
void showAvailableTests(covdbHandle designHandle)
{
covdbHandle tn, tns;
tns = covdb_iterate(designHandle, covdbAvailableTests);
printf("Design: %s has the following tests:\n",
covdb_get_str(designHandle, covdbName));
while(tn = covdb_scan(tns))
printf("\ttest: %s\n", covdb_get_str(tn, covdbName));
covdb_release_handle(tns);
}
Loading Tests
Tests are not automatically loaded with a design. Once a design handle has been
obtained, the handle is used to access the list of test names and then load one or more of
the tests. A single test is loaded with covdb_load while two or more tests can be loaded
with successive calls to covdb_loadmerge.
Loading a single test requires that a valid design handle and test name string be available:
/* load a single test from a design */
covdbHandle testHandle;
testHandle = covdb_load(covdbTest, designHandle, testName);
if(!testHandle) {
printf("UCAPI Error: could not load test %s\n", testName);
exit(1);
}
Loading and merging two or more tests first requires the loading of a single test with
covdb_load followed by multiple calls to covdb_loadmerge to load and merge the
additional tests' data. The following example shows a function used to load all the
available tests related to a design. It is assumed that all of the test data is contained in one
location with the design data.
covdbHandle loadTests(covdbHandle designHandle)
{
covdbHandle tn, tns;
covdbHandle mergedTest;

// get an iterator to the list of availableTests
tns = covdb_iterate(designHandle, covdbAvailableTests);

// Using the iterator get the first available test
tn = covdb_scan(tns);

// load the first test
mergedTest = covdb_load(covdbTest, designHandle,
covdb_get_str(tn, covdbName));
if(mergedTest)
printf("Successfully loaded test %0s\n",
covdb_get_str(tn, covdbName);

Loading a Design
{
printf("Unable to load test\n");
return NULL;
}

// using loadmerge load and merge the remaining tests

while((tn = covdb_scan(tns)))
{
mergedTest = covdb_loadmerge(covdbTest, mergedTest,
covdb_get_str(tn, covdbName));
if(!mergedTest)
printf("Could not load test %s\n",
covdb_get_str(tn, covdbName));
printf("Successfully loaded and merged test %s\n",
covdb_get_str(tn,covdbName);
}
covdb_release_handle(tns);
return mergedTest;
}

