atching different policy classes when you instantiate
the host class template. If you need a behavior not included in the library, write a custom
policy implementation. Use this approach to support situations the library designer did not
anticipate.
The base classes of the VMM Performance Analyzer now have parameterization support
with the default parameters being vmm_data and the default policy class being the VMM
policy class.
class vmm_data_object_policy;
static function bit compare (vmm_data actual, vmm_data expected, ref
string diff);
return actual.compare(expected,diff);
static function void display (vmm_data trans, string str = "");
trans.display(str);
static function string psdisplay (vmm_data trans, string str = "");
return trans.psdisplay(str);
endclass : vmm_data_object_policy

class vmm_perf_tenure #(type T =vmm_data, object_policy =
vmm_data_object_policy); ..
Endclass

class vmm_perf_analyzer_callbacks #(type T =vmm_data, object_policy =
vmm_data_object_policy); ..
Endclass

class vmm_perf_analyzer #(type T =vmm_data, object_policy =
vmm_data_object_policy);
Therefore, it maintains complete backward compatibility with the previous non-
parameterized mode as far as the default specialization is concerned and no new
parameters are passed.

To use it with UVM or OVM, you need to create the respective policy class and pass that
as a parameter:
A typical object policy class for a UVM transaction xbus_transfer is shown below:
class xbus_object_policy;
static function string psdisplay (xbus_transfer trans, string str =
"");
trans.print();
endclass : xbus_object_policy
Here, the implementation of the display routine is provided as a static function.
In addition, you need to provide the specialization of the UVM transaction type and the
UVM policy to PA base classes.
For example, when instantiating the PA,
vmm_perf_analyzer #(xbus_transfer, xbus_object_policy) xbus_perf;
When specializing a tenure,
vmm_perf_tenure #(xbus_transfer, xbus_object_policy) tenure;
The following steps define the integration of PA in an UVM environment. The xbus
example in $VCS_HOME/doc/examples/uvm/simple/xbus is used as an example.
1. Creating the policy class for UVM and defining its policies.
Data is collected for each resource in a separate instance of the vmm_perf_analyzer
class. These instances should be allocated in the build phase of the top level
environment. Inside xbus_demo_tb environment class, a handle of the PA is
appropriately specialized as mentioned earlier.
class xbus_demo_tb extends uvm_env;

// Provide implementations of virtual methods such as get_type_name
and create
`uvm_component_utils(xbus_demo_tb)

// xbus environment
xbus_env xbus0;

// Scoreboard to check the memory operation of the slave.
xbus_demo_scoreboard scoreboard0;

// vmm_perf_analyzer's instances
vmm_perf_analyzer #(xbus_transfer, xbus_object_policy) xbus_perf;
