Chapter 1: RTL Coding Guidelines for Better Performance With X-Propagation
Functions With Multiple Return Statement
execution of flow control statement needs to consider subsequent loop iterations as well, it
is recommended that wherever possible you should rewrite such loops as a priority case.
Original Coding Style
for (int i = 0; i < SIZE; i++) begin
if (req_raised[i]) begin
sel[i] = 1'b1;
break;
end
end
Recommended Coding Style
priority case (1'b1)
req_raised[0]: sel[0] = 1'b1;
req_raised[1]: sel[1] = 1'b1;
req_raised[2]: sel[2] = 1'b1;
req_raised[3]: sel[3] = 1'b1;
endcase
Functions With Multiple Return Statement
Like loops with flow control, functions with multiple return statement in Xprop simulation
need to consider multiple exit points and thus could lower performance.
It is recommended that wherever possible, you should rewrite the function with multiple
returns to a function with single/no return.
Original Coding Style
function automatic reg [7:0] isGated(logic noGatingAllowed, logic
locked, logic idle);
if (noGatingAllowed)
return 0;
if (locked && idle)
return 1;
return 0;
endfunction
Recommended Coding Style
function automatic reg [7:0] canGate(logic noGatingAllowed, logic
locked, logic idle);
if (noGatingAllowed)
canGate = 0;
if (locked && idle)
canGate = 1;
canGate = 0;
endfunction
