Instantiating a VHDL Design Entity With a Variable Port WidthVHDLinstantiating top-level design entitywith a variable port width
In VHDL, you can define a port of variable width by specifying its width using a generic.
Doing so enables you to pass a size to the port in the generic map of a component
instantiation statement. This technique enables a port to have different widths in different
instances of the component. Example 51 shows an entity declaration with a port with a
variable width.
Example 51 A VHDL Entity With A Variable Port Width
entity dev is
generic (size : integer := 4);
port (inport : in std_logic;
outport : out std_logic_vector(0 to size-1));
end dev;
You can instantiate this design entity like any other design entity and pass a new size to
the port as a parameter in the module instantiation statement.
Example 52 Passing A New Port Width as a Parameter
dev #(8)vhdldev1 (reg1, wire8bit);
Instantiating a Signed or Unsigned VHDL Design Entity
You can instantiate a VHDL entity having a port or generic of type signed or unsigned in a
Verilog module. VHDL explicitly supports signed and unsigned types, and Verilog supports
arithmetic and arithmetic shift operations on signed vectors of arbitrary length. Further,
though Verilog does not have any explicit unsigned types, all wire types are effectively
unsigned. VHDL explicitly supports signed and unsigned types.
The following example shows a VHDL adder design with signed and unsigned types
followed by the part of the Verilog testbench design instantiating the VHDL module.
Example 53 Instantiating Signed and Unsigned VHDL Design Entity
VHDL design:
library IEEE;
use IEEE.STD_LOGIC_1164.all;
use IEEE.std_logic_arith.all;

entity FULL_ADD8 is
port (A: in signed(7 downto 0);
B: in unsigned(7 downto 0);
CIN: in std_logic;
SUM: out signed(7 downto 0));
end FULL_ADD8;

architecture FOR_GENERATE of FULL_ADD8 is

process (A, B, CIN)
SUM <= A + B + CIN;
end FOR_GENERATE;

configuration CFG_fulladder of FULL_ADD8 is
for FOR_GENERATE
end for;
Verilog testbench:
// verilog: signed + unsigned
// vhdl: signed + unsigned
`timescale 1 ns / 1 ns

wire signed [7:0] SUM;
reg signed [7:0] A;
reg [7:0] B;
reg CIN;

FULL_ADD8 i1(A, B, CIN, SUM);

A = 4'b0;
B = 4'b0;
CIN = 1'b0;
#1 A = -4'sh3;
B = 4'b0011;
#1 A = 4'b0010;
B = 4'sh7;
.
.
.
end
