Enabling Feature Filtering With keep and remove
You can use the keep and remove keywords to control which features are visible in your
verification plan. These keywords help you customize your plan by hiding or affirming
features based on specific criteria. This is useful for focusing on relevant features,
simplifying your plan view, and improving verification efficiency.
The filtered plan shows only the features that remain visible after applying the filter rules.
This section includes the following topics:
• Using the remove Keyword
• Using the keep Keyword
• Combining the remove and keep Keywords
• Using Multiple Filter Groups
Using the remove Keyword
The remove keyword hides features that match the specified criteria. Features that do not
match remain unchanged.
filter my_filter;
remove feature where (priority == "low");
In the above example, features with low priority are hidden. Others stay visible.
Using the keep Keyword
The keep keyword affirms the visibility for features that match the criteria. Non-matching
features are not hidden by keep.
The keep keyword inside a filter works only when used with remove
instructions. Using keep alone does not change the filter result.
filter my_filter;
keep feature where (owner == "XYZ");
In the above example, features owned by XYZ are affirmed as visible. Others remain
unchanged.

Combining the remove and keep Keywords
The order of remove and keep lines matters. The last action applied to a feature
determines its visibility.
filter my_filter;
remove feature where (priority == "low");
keep feature where (owner == "XYZ");
In the above example, features with low priority are hidden, but features owned by XYZ are
rescued and shown again.
Using Multiple Filter Groups
You can use several filter groups in one file. Each group acts independently.
filter group1;
remove feature where (category == "test");

filter group2;
keep feature where (owner == "ABC");

filter group3;
remove feature where (status != "done");

filter group4;
keep feature where (score > 80);

filter group5;
remove feature where (count < 10);
In the above example, the following happens:
• Features matching test are hidden in group1.
• Features owned by ABC are affirmed in group2.
• Features that are not completed are hidden in group3.
• Features with a score greater than 80 are affirmed in group4.
• Features with a count less than 10 are hidden in group5.

until Statement – Time-Based Modifier
As a project develops, you can specify a time-based selective block, using an until
statement, to apply different override or filter rules. The until elseuntil
enduntil block can wrap override and filter statements, and only the block that
meets the current time conditions is applied to the verification plan. The syntax of the
until statement is:
until date-format;
override-specification | filter-specification;
{ elseuntil date-format;
override-specification | filter-specification; }
{ else;
override-specification | filter-specification; }
enduntil
date-format ::= INT "-" INT "-" INT
until 1-31-2014;
/* applied before 1/31/2014 */
override relaxed_group_goal;
new.top.Group = Group > 40%;
new.top.group_expected = 0.4;
elseuntil 04-30-2014;
/* applied between 2/1/2014 - 4/30/2014 */
override relaxed_group_goal;
new.top.Group = Group > 60%;
new.top.group_expected = 0.6;
else;
/* applied after 5/1/2014 */
override relaxed_group_goal;
new.top.Group = Group >= 100%;
new.top.group_expected = 1.0;
enduntil
Verification Planner reports the total number of coverpoints or cover bins in covergroup
definitions. For this, Verification Planner provides three new built-in metrics,
Group.grp_count, Group.cvp_count, and Group.bin_count. These three metrics return
integer values and can be specified in any measure statement. For example:
feature f1;
measure Group, Group.grp_count Group.cvp_count Group.bin_count
Measure_1;

source = "group: top.b1::cg::SHAPE{p=1}";
measure Group, Group.grp_count Group.cvp_count Group.bin_count
Measure_2;
source = "group: top.b1::cg::SHAPE{p=1}.cp1";
The score of measure total is calculated as the aggregate of all measures. In the following
example, the top.b1::cg::SHAPE{p=1}.cp1 coverpoint is counted two times. The total
score of Group.cvp_count is 2, as one score is assigned for each time.
Measure Totals:
SCORE GROUP Group.grp_count Group.cvp_count Group.bin_count

Sources:
group: top.b1::cg::SHAPE{p=1}
SCORE GROUP Group.grp_count Group.cvp_count Group.bin_count NAME

The following is an example of an HVP plan written to measure the toggle coverage of
individual code coverage objects in a specific module and instance:
feature Feature_2;
measure Toggle M_2;
source = "module : cntrlr@bus*";
source = "module : cntrlr@cl?";
measure Toggle bitSelect;
source = "module : cntrlr@busAddr[7:1]";
source = "module : cntrlr@busAddr[1][0->1]";
To annotate the scores, run the hvp annotate command with xml plan , covdb,
userdata, and other required options as follows:
% hvp annotate -plan <plan_name>.xml -dir simv.vdb
To generate the URG report, execute the following command:
% urg -dir simv.vdb -format both -plan <plan_name>.hvp
Examples
Case 1: Using the signal name as object name.
module cntrlr(clk, reset, busAddr, busData, busRdWr_N, adxStrb, rdWr_N,
ce0_N,ce1_N, ce2_N, ce3_N, ramAddr, ramData);

input clk, reset, busRdWr_N, adxStrb;
input [7:0] busAddr;
inout [7:0] busData;
output rdWr_N, ce0_N, ce1_N, ce2_N, ce3_N;
output [5:0] ramAddr;
inout [7:0] ramData;
