entity TB is
end TB;

architecture TB_A of TB is
component counter

port (clk : in bit; d0, d1, d2, d3 : inout std_logic);
end component;

signal clk : bit := '0';
signal d0, d1, d2, d3 : std_logic := '0';

P1: process (clk)
constant delay : time := 5 ns;
clk <= not clk after delay;
end process;

C1 : counter
port map (clk, d0, d1, d2, d3);
C2 : counter
port map (clk, d0, d1, d2, d3);

end TB_A;
Hierarchy Access
The entity-architecture pair TB(TB_A) is a root instance containing two component
instance statements, C1 and C2, and a process statement, P1.
The following C code accesses the hierarchy:
/* create an iterator for the root instances */
vhpiHandleT rootInstances = vhpi_iterator(vhpiRootInsts, NULL);

vhpiHandleT region;
vhpiHandleT instance;
vhpiHandleT internalRegions;

/* iterate over the root instances */
while (instance = vhpi_scan(rootInstances))
{
/* create an iterator for the internal regions, */
/* this would be the set {C1, C2, P1} */
internalRegions = vhpi_iterator(vhpiInternalRegions, rootInstance);
if (internalRegions) /* there are internal regions */
{
/* iterate over the internal regions */
while (region = vhpi_scan(internalRegions))
{
/* get the pathname of the region and print it */
char pathName[MAX_STR];
char classKind[MAX_STR];

sprintf(pathname, "The path_name of a <%s>",
vhpi_get_str(vhpiFullNameP, region));
sprintf(classKind, "region is %s\n",
vhpi_get_str(vhpiKindStrP, region));
vhpi_release_handle(region);
}
vhpi_release_handle(internalRegions);
}
vhpi_release_handle(rootInstance);
}
vhpi_release_handle(rootInstances);
The output of this program fragment is
The path_name of a <vhpiCompInstStmtK> region is :TB:C1
The path_name of a <vhpiCompInstStmtK> region is :TB:C2
The path_name of a <vhpiProcessStmtK> region is :TB:P1
Declarations Access
The declarations apply only to regions. With a reference handle to a region, VHPI
provides a one-to-many relationship called vhpiDecls which can be used to access the
declarations in scope. The declarations can be type declarations or object declarations.
The objects declarations are constants, variables, signals, generics, ports, files. The
following example describes access to the declarations within the root instance of the
VHDL code above.
/* get a handle to the process P1 inside the root instance */
vhpiHandleT processHandle, processDecls;

processDecls = vhpi_iterator(vhpiDecls, processHandle);
if (processDecls) /* there are declarations in */
/* the process region */
{
vhpiHandleT decl;
while (decl = vhpi_scan(processDecls))
{
char pathName[MAX_STR];
char classKind[MAX_STR];

sprintf(classKind, "The declaration <%s>",
vhpi_get_str(vhpiKindStrP, decl));
sprintf(pathname, "has a path_name of a %s\n",
vhpi_get_str(vhpiFullNameP, decl));

vhpi_release_handle(decl);
}
vhpi_release_handle(processDecls);

}
vhpi_release_handle(processHandle);
The program fragment above outputs
The declaration <vhpiConstDeclK> has a path_name of :TB:P1:DELAY
If you can get a handle to the root and use it to iterate over the declarations, you have a
list that consists of the following sequence: CLK, D0, D1, D2 and D3, they are all of type
vhpiSigDeclK.
To iterate over the ports of a component, such as C1, you can use the following code
fragment:
vhpiHandleT ports, component, port;

component = vhpi_handle_by_name(":TB:C1", NULL);
ports = vhpi_iterator(vhpiPortDecls, component);

while (port = vhpi_scan(ports))
{
vhpi_printf("Port <%s> has mode %d\n",
vhpi_get_str(vhpiNameP, port),
vhpi_get(vhpiModeP, port));

vhpi_release_handle(port);
}
vhpi_release_handle(ports);
vhpi_release_handle(component);
The enumerated type vhpiModeT has values 0 for vhpiIn, 1 for vhpiOut, and 2
for vhpiInout.
The output of the program fragment above is
Port CLK has mode 0
Port D0 has mode 2
Port D1 has mode 2
Port D2 has mode 2
Port D3 has mode 2
Types Access
Consider the following VHDL code fragment to illustrate the type access.
type MSG_TYPES is (no_msg, info_1, warn_1, err_1, debug_1, debug_2,
debug_mem1, debug_mem2);
subtype DEBUG_MSG is MSG_TYPES range debug_1 to debug_mem2;
subtype MEM_MSG is DEBUG_MSG range debug_mem1 to debug_mem2;

signal MSG_LEVEL : MEM_MSG;

Assuming that MSG_LEVEL is declared within the instance :TB:C1:C2:C3, you can use
the following code fragment to traverse the type hierarchy of MSG_LEVEL.
vhpiHandleT msgLevel, subtype, basetype;
vhpiHandleT literals, literal;
char* baseTypeSimpleName;
int typeKind;

/* get a handle by doing a name lookup */
msgLevel = vhpi_handle_by_name(":TB:C1:C2:C3:MSG_LEVEL", NULL);

/* get the subtype of signal msgLevel.
This will be a subtype indication (always) */
subtype = vhpi_handle(vhpiSubtype, msgLevel);

basetype = vhpi_handle(vhpiBaseType, msgLevel);

baseTypeSimpleName = vhpi_get_str(vhpiNameP, basetype);
/* will be MSG_TYPES */

typeKind = vhpi_get(vhpiKindP, baseType);
/* will be vhpiEnumTypeDeclK */

/* in order to iterate over the literals */
literals = vhpi_iterator(vhpiEnumLiterals, basetype);

vhpi_printf("The enum literals are \n");
while (literal = vhpi_scan(literals))
{
vhpi_printf("%s, ", vhpi_get_str(vhpiNameP, literal);
vhpi_release_handle(literal);
}
vhpi_release_handle(literals);
vhpi_release_handle(basetype);
vhpi_release_handle(subtype);
vhpi_release_handle(msgLevel);
The code fragment above prints
The enum literals are
NO_MSG, INFO_1, WARN_1, ERR_1, DEBUG_1, DEBUG_2, DEBUG_MEM1,
DEBUG_MEM2
The subtype handle obtained above for MSG_LEVEL corresponds to a subtype indication.
For all objects, the one-to-one relationship that leads to the subtype returns the subtype
indication. From the subtype indication handle, which is the subtype above, you can get
the typemark, which corresponds to the subtype declaration DEBUG_MSG. The range of
the subtype indication can be obtained using the vhpiConstraints relation. The range
can be used to get the left bound, which corresponds to "debug_mem1" and the right
bound corresponds to "debug_mem2". The IsUpP boolean property is true.
Considering the C code above, the following fragment

char childName[VHPI_MID_STRING_SIZE];
strcpy(childName, vhpi_get_str(vhpiNameP, msgLevel));

while (subtype)
{
vhpi_printf("The subtype of %s is %s\n",
childName, vhpi_get_str(vhpiNameP, subtype));

strcpy(childName, vhpi_get_str(vhpiNameP, subtype));
subtype = vhpi_handle(vhpiTypeMark, subtype);
}
generates the following output:
The subtype of MSG_LEVEL is MEM_MSG
The typemark of MEM_MSG is DEBUG_MSG
The typemark of DEBUG_MSG is MSG_TYPES

Dynamic Value Access
You can access the dynamic information of a design to
• Get the value of an object
• Perform an immediate update on the value of an object
To perform these two operations, VHPI defines the following access functions to get and
set the value of objects:
int vhpi_value_size(vhpiHandleT h, vhpiFormatT format);
int vhpi_get_value(vhpiHandleT h, vhpiValueT* v);
int vhpi_put_value(vhpiHandleT h, vhpiValueT* v, int flags);
int vhpi_schedule_transaction(vhpiHandleT h, vhpiValueT**
vv, int num, vhpiTimeT* delay, int mode,
vhpiTimeT*pulse);
h: Is a handle to an object that possesses a value.
v: Is a pointer to a value data structure you allocated.
flags: Indicates the semantics of the immediate signal updates and it can be either
vhpiForce, vhpiForcePropagate, vhpiForceHold, or vhpiForceHoldPropagate.
vv: Is a pointer to an array of pointers of value data structures you allocated to schedule
transactions.
num: Specifies size of vv array.
delay: Specifies a transaction delay.
mode: Specifies delay mode (vhpiInertial, vhpiTransport)
pulse: Is a pulse rejection limit. (It is not supported in this version, it may have any value.)
The vhpi_get_value() function is described in the Value Access Function section. The
vhpi_schedule_transaction() and thevhpi_put_value() functions are described in
the Value Modification Function section.
