C Initialization Function
Write the C initialization function to perform the following tasks:
1. Gets a handle for the input port of the invertor gate (port1).
2. Gets a handle for the output port of the invertor gate (port2).
3. Allocates value data structure for each port and specifies the format of the data to be
read and written to the value data structure. Since the input and the output ports for the
invertor gate are of type std_logic, the format is specified as vhpiEnumVal.
4. Reads the initial value of the input port, evaluates the output port (using eval_inv()
routine), and then writes back the value of the output port.
5. Allocates a callback data structure and fills in the fields with the required data. The
callback reason is vhpiCbValueChange on the input port.
6. Registers the callback so it triggers every time there is a change of value on the input
port.
When the callback fires, it calls the callback function that in turns calls the evaluation
function that has the functionality of the invertor gate. The callback function then compares
the new value of the output with the old value and if they are different, it writes back to the
output port.
The complete C code for the initialization function is listed below.
void inv_init(vhpiHandleT compInst)
{
vhpiHandleT port1;
vhpiCbDataT *p1CbData;

/* Get the first port */
port1 = vhpi_handle_by_index(vhpiPortDecls, compInst, 0);
if (vhpi_get(vhpiModeP, port1) != vhpiIn) {
vhpi_get_str(vhpiFullNameP, port1));
}

/* Get the second port */
port2 = vhpi_handle_by_index(vhpiPortDecls, compInst, 1);
if (vhpi_get(vhpiModeP, port2) != vhpiOut) {
vhpi_printf("Error: Port '%s' is not of mode out\n",
vhpi_get_str(vhpiFullNameP, port2));
}

/* Allocate value-structures for the ports */
value1 = (vhpiValueT*) malloc(sizeof(vhpiValueT));

2-Input Nand Gate (NAND2)
value1->format = vhpiEnumVal;

value2 = (vhpiValueT*) malloc(sizeof(vhpiValueT));
value2->format = vhpiEnumVal;

/* Get the values of i/p's and evaluate o/p */
vhpi_get_value(port1, value1);
value2->value.enumval = eval_inv(value1->value.enumval);
vhpi_put_value(port2, value2, vhpiForcePropagate);

/* Setup callback on i/p port1 */
p1CbData = (vhpiCbDataT*) malloc(sizeof(vhpiCbDataT));
p1CbData->reason = vhpiCbValueChange;
p1CbData->obj = port1;
p1CbData->value = value1;
p1CbData->time = (vhpiTimeT*) malloc(sizeof(vhpiTimeT));
p1CbData->cbf = value_change_callback_on_inv_input;
vhpi_register_cb(p1CbData);
}
2-Input Nand Gate (NAND2)
The entity-architecture definitions the C behavior for the 2-input nand gate are described
here.
VHDL Entity-Architecture Declarations
The entity-architecture definitions for the gate behavior are shown below. Note that the
architecture body is empty of simulation code and uses the FOREIGN attribute to flag the
simulator that the actual behavior is defined in a C foreign model.
The VHDL shell for the nand gate is
library Synopsys, IEEE;
use IEEE.std_logic_1164.all;
use Synopsys.attributes.all;

entity nand2 is
port (a, b : in std_logic; y : out std_logic);
end nand2;

architecture A of nand2 is
attribute FOREIGN of A : architecture is
"vhpi:nand2-O:nand2_elab:nand2_init:NAND2";
end A;

configuration cfg_nand2 of nand2 is
for a

2-Input Nand Gate (NAND2)
end cfg_nand2;
The FOREIGN attribute in the architecture body tells the simulator that the C behavior of
the nand gate is compiled into the libnand2-O.so shared library, the elaboration function
name is nand2_elab(), and the initialization function name is nand2_init().
Writing the C Behavior Code
Writing the C behavior code includes writing the C elaboration function and the C
initialization function.
